<div class="main">
<header class="intro">
<nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="crumbs"><a href="/al-fx-quadgt">al-fx-quadgt</a> / User guide</nav>
<h1><span class="nowrap">al-fx-quadgt</span> user guide</h1>
<p class="lede">al-fx-quadgt runs the QuadraVerb GT's own firmware, v1.03: what you know from a GT applies here, and its owner's manual describes the programs, the edit pages and the effects. This guide covers what the plugin adds or leaves out. It's an effect: put it on a track or bus with audio, a guitar recorded direct or anything else. Installing is on the <a href="/download#installing">download page</a>; the ROM folder, artwork files, the update check and contact are on <a href="/support">Support</a>.</p>
<ol class="toc">
{toc.map(({ id, title }) => <li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}
</ol>
</header>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="start">
<div class="eyebrow">1</div>
<h2>Getting started</h2>

<h3 id="first-start">The first start</h3>
<p>The firmware, with the effects and the factory programs in it, is Alesis's copyrighted work, so Lemus doesn't include it, and without it al-fx-quadgt has nothing to run. The first time it starts without its ROMs it asks for them: <span class="k">Locate ROM…</span> takes a file, a zip holding it, or a folder. A file is checked and copied into the ROM folder; a folder becomes the ROM folder.</p>
<p>Then the firmware boots, as the unit does when it's switched on. The first time, it loads its 90 factory programs the way the unit does it, which takes a few seconds; after that it starts from the memory the session keeps.</p>

<h3 id="roms">The ROMs</h3>
<p>Two files, found by checksum anywhere in the <a href="/support#rom">ROM folder</a>, whatever they're called: the firmware, v1.03 or v1.00 (v1.03 if both are there), and the character ROM.</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">Size</th><th scope="col">SHA1</th><th scope="col">Holds</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="mono">qvgtv103.bin</td><td class="num">65,536 bytes</td><td class="mono">0ac063e74b6c6ecf9edda10824df9a79c621ccd9</td><td>The 27C512 EPROM (U30) as read from the chip: firmware v1.03, the effects and the factory programs</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">qvgtv100.bin</td><td class="num">65,536 bytes</td><td class="mono">f09ee9d50425a407fa33b6ac8eb79eca660e491f</td><td>Firmware v1.00 (1991), in v1.03's place: the first factory set, no global cabinet simulator controls, and the two faults v1.01 fixed</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">hd44780_a00.bin</td><td class="num">4,096 bytes</td><td class="mono">65cf075a988cdcbb316b9afdd0529b374a1a65ec</td><td>The display controller's character ROM, as in MAME's sets (the DX7's, among others): the display is drawn from it. The older copy drawn from the datasheet (SHA1 bc0cdf0c9ba895f22e183c7bd35a3f655f2ca96f) works too: its six wrong characters are corrected</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>The memory a session keeps is the firmware's it was saved with. Opened with the other firmware, the unit starts with that one's factory programs, and the session keeps the saved memory for when its firmware is back.</p>

<h3 id="artwork">Panel artwork</h3>
<p>The two blank places take an SVG or PNG of your own each, named as below, anywhere in the ROM folder (<a href="/support#artwork">how artwork files work</a>). <span class="k">ROMs</span> in the top bar lists them with what each has, opens the folder with Show its folder, and reads it again with Rescan.</p>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="The panel with the two blank artwork places outlined at the top: maker on the left, name over the display" marks={[{ ...at.maker, n: 'maker' }, { ...at.name, n: 'name' }]} caption="The two places." />
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">Place</th><th scope="col">Size</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="mono">al-fx-quadgt-maker.svg or .png</td><td>Alesis logo</td><td>27.7 × 4.9 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">al-fx-quadgt-name.svg or .png</td><td>QuadraVerb GT logo</td><td>66.6 × 8.4 mm</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>The plain names (<span class="mono">maker.svg</span>…) in <span class="path">artwork/qvgt/</span> or <span class="mono">artwork/qvgt.zip</span> work too, and win over these.</p>

<h3 id="first-sound">Your first sound</h3>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="INPUT, the display, VALUE and PROG outlined" crop={{ x: 120, y: 0, w: 680, h: 114 }} marks={[{ ...at.input, n: 1 }, { ...at.lcd, n: 2 }, { ...at.value, n: 3 }, { ...at.prog, n: 4 }]} />
<ul>
<li>Play audio through the track: a new instance is on program 00, GUITARBYALESIS.</li>
<li>Turn INPUT <Num n="1" /> until the −6 dB LED lights on the loudest notes, as the owner's manual sets it. INPUT is the unit's own knob: it drives the preamp, so it changes the sound, not only the level.</li>
<li>With the display <Num n="2" /> showing a program, VALUE <Num n="3" /> steps through them; PROG <Num n="4" /> goes back to the program from an edit page. From a DAW, a program change does the same.</li>
<li>The edit keys open their pages (REVERB, DELAY, PITCH, EQ, MIDI, CONFIG, MIX, NAME/MOD, PRE-AMP), PAGE moves through a page's settings and VALUE changes them, as the manual describes.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="panel">
<div class="eyebrow">2</div>
<h2>The panel</h2>
<p>It is the unit's, measured from photos: every key goes through the firmware's own key scan, every LED shows what the firmware lights, and the display shows the controller's dots from its character ROM.</p>

<h3 id="memory">Programs and memory</h3>
<ul>
<li>The programs are in the unit's battery-backed RAM: the 90 factory programs at first, and anything you STORE <Num n="1" /> over them, as on the unit.</li>
<li>The session keeps all of it: see <a href="#automation">Automation and sessions</a>.</li>
<li>State, Reset… in the top bar makes a new instance: the factory programs again.</li>
</ul>
<Shot src={panel} base={base} alt="STORE outlined" crop={{ x: 560, y: 0, w: 420, h: 114 }} marks={[{ ...at.store, n: 1 }]} />

<h3 id="force">Key force</h3>
<p>The unit's keys sense how hard they're pressed: the firmware scrolls VALUE faster the harder it's held. A click or a computer key is a light press; with Shift held it's a hard one.</p>

<h3 id="levels">INPUT and the LEDs</h3>
<p>Levels are volts at the unit's jacks: 0 dBFS in the DAW is +4 dBu, a hot line level. A guitar recorded direct usually needs INPUT well up. The four LEDs follow the preamp's input as the unit's do; all four light when the DSP overflows. HISS on the bottom bar adds the parts' own noise, as a unit has.</p>

<h3 id="power">POWER</h3>
<p>The POWER switch turns the unit off and on. Off, it's silent and dark, and its memory is kept; on, it boots from it, as the unit does. A new instance or a session load doesn't need it: the plugin starts on.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="rear">
<div class="eyebrow">3</div>
<h2>The rear</h2>
<p><span class="k">REAR</span> in the bottom bar turns the unit round.</p>
<Shot src={rear} base={base} alt="The rear panel: POWER 9V AC, the ADVANCE and BYPASS footswitch jacks, MIDI THRU/OUT and IN, OUTPUT L and R, EFFECTS RETURN and SEND, INPUT L and R" marks={[{ ...jacks(68.1, 68.1), n: 1 }, { ...jacks(97.9, 119.2), n: 2 }, { ...jacks(143.9, 164.5), n: 3 }, { ...jacks(189.1, 209.7), n: 4 }, { ...jacks(241.4, 263.2), n: 5 }, { ...jacks(391.8, 412.4), n: 6 }]} />

<h3 id="audio">INPUT and OUTPUT</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">INPUT L and R</span> <Num n="6" /> are the plugin's input; a mono track feeds both. The preamp takes their sum, as the unit's does, and the dry path keeps them apart.</li>
<li><span class="k">OUTPUT L and R</span> <Num n="4" /> are its output, always stereo.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="effects-loop">EFFECTS</h3>
<p>The effects loop's SEND and RETURN <Num n="5" /> take no cable: the send is computed but goes nowhere, and the return is as with nothing plugged in. POWER <Num n="1" /> is the socket for the unit's adapter.</p>

<h3 id="footswitches">Footswitches</h3>
<p>Click <span class="k">BYPASS</span> or <span class="k">ADVANCE</span> <Num n="2" /> for a footswitch press: BYPASS switches the effects out and in, ADVANCE steps to the next program, as the manual describes. MIDI IN and THRU/OUT <Num n="3" /> take no cable either: MIDI comes from the DAW (<a href="#midi">MIDI and your DAW</a>), and THRU isn't emulated.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="bars">
<div class="eyebrow">4</div>
<h2>The bars</h2>

<h3 id="top-bar">The top bar</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">BETA</span> after the name: this version is a beta. About shows the version.</li>
<li><span class="k">ROMs:</span> the ROM folder, the ROMs and the <a href="#artwork">artwork</a>, with their full paths, and Show, Change… and Rescan for the folder.</li>
<li><span class="k">State:</span> see <a href="#state">State</a>.</li>
<li><span class="k">About:</span> the version, the ROM folder and artwork in use, the <a href="/support#updates">update check</a>, and links to this guide, the site and the forum. A newer version shows as a button in the bar.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="state">State</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">Save to file…</span> saves the instance: the knobs, HISS and the battery RAM (every program, the edits, the global settings).</li>
<li><span class="k">Load from file…</span> loads one. The standalone app's Options menu saves and loads the same files.</li>
<li><span class="k">Reset…</span> makes it a new instance: the factory programs, every knob at its default.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="bottom-bar">The bottom bar</h3>
<dl class="legend">
<div><dt>HISS</dt><dd>The parts' own noise, as on a unit: the op-amps', the VCAs' and the converter's (on by default). Off, the plugin is noiseless and settles to exact zeros.</dd></div>
<div><dt>REAR</dt><dd>The rear panel: <a href="#rear">The rear</a>.</dd></div>
<div><dt>?</dt><dd>Shows the list of computer keys over the panel. Any click, ? or Escape closes it.</dd></div>
</dl>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="midi">
<div class="eyebrow">5</div>
<h2>MIDI and your DAW</h2>

<h3 id="midi-in">MIDI in</h3>
<p>The DAW's MIDI goes to the firmware through the unit's MIDI IN, byte by byte, and it does with it what the unit does: program changes, controllers and sysex, on the channel its MIDI page sets. A program change picks a program, as the manual's MIDI chapter describes.</p>

<h3 id="midi-out">MIDI out</h3>
<p>What the unit sends from MIDI OUT comes out of the plugin's MIDI output, such as the dumps its MIDI page sends. Route it to another track to record it.</p>

<h3 id="automation">Automation and sessions</h3>
<p>Three parameters can be automated: Input and Output, the panel's knobs, and Hiss. Everything else goes by the panel or MIDI.</p>
<p>A session saves them and the battery RAM, as <a href="#state">State</a> does; the instance boots from them when the session opens.</p>
<p>After 1.6 s of silence the DSP and the preamp sleep; the 80C31 keeps running, so the display and LEDs carry on. Sound at the input, a key or MIDI wakes it. With HISS on, the hiss goes on while it sleeps. al-fx-quadgt reports a 30 s tail.</p>
</section>

<!-- ===================================================================== -->
<section id="keyboard">
<div class="eyebrow">6</div>
<h2>Keyboard and mouse</h2>

<h3 id="keys">Computer keys</h3>
<p>Click the panel first.</p>
<div class="scroll">
<table>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Keys</th><th scope="col">Panel</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="mono">Left, Right</td><td>PAGE down, up</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Down, Up</td><td>VALUE down, up</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Return</td><td>STORE</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">P</td><td>PROG</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">B</td><td>BYPASS</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Shift with a key</td><td>A hard press</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">?</td><td>The key list</td></tr>
<tr><td class="mono">Escape</td><td>Closes the key list</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Some DAWs keep letter keys for themselves.</p>

<h3 id="mouse">Mouse</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="k">Knobs:</span> drag up or down; shift is finer, double-click goes back to the start position.</li>
<li><span class="k">Keys</span> are held while the button is; shift-click is a hard press.</li>
<li><span class="k">Window:</span> drag the bottom-right corner to resize it.</li>
</ul>
</section>

</div>

</div>
</div>

</Base>

<script>
// The sidebar marks the heading being read and opens its section; the button opens it as a
// drawer on a narrow window. Without this script the sidebar shows every heading.
const nav = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.side')!;
const button = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('.toc-button')!;
const links = [...nav.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('a')];
const targets = links.map((a) => document.getElementById(a.hash.slice(1))!);
nav.classList.add('spy');
button.hidden = false;

let current = -1;
function mark() {
  let i = -1;
  targets.forEach((t, j) => { if (t.getBoundingClientRect().top <= 120) i = j; });
  // At the end of the page the last headings can't reach the top: take the one jumped to, if
  // it's in view, or else the last.
  if (innerHeight + scrollY >= document.documentElement.scrollHeight - 2) {
    const jumped = targets.findIndex((t) => '#' + t.id === location.hash);
    i = jumped >= 0 && targets[jumped].getBoundingClientRect().top < innerHeight ? jumped : targets.length - 1;
  }
  if (i === current) return;
  current = i;
  links.forEach((a, j) => {
    a.classList.toggle('on', j === i);
    if (j === i) a.setAttribute('aria-current', 'location'); else a.removeAttribute('aria-current');
  });
  const section = i >= 0 ? links[i].closest('.side > ol > li') : null;
  for (const li of nav.querySelectorAll(':scope > ol > li')) li.classList.toggle('open', li === section);
  reveal();
}

// Keep the marked heading in view when the sidebar is taller than the window.
function reveal() {
  const a = links[current];
  if (!a || nav.scrollHeight <= nav.clientHeight) return;
  if (a.offsetTop < nav.scrollTop || a.offsetTop + a.offsetHeight > nav.scrollTop + nav.clientHeight)
    nav.scrollTop = a.offsetTop - nav.clientHeight / 3;
}

let frame = 0;
const later = () => { if (!frame) frame = requestAnimationFrame(() => { frame = 0; mark(); }); };
addEventListener('scroll', later, { passive: true });
addEventListener('resize', later);
addEventListener('hashchange', later);
mark();

function open(on: boolean) {
  nav.classList.toggle('open', on);
  button.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(on));
  button.querySelector('span')!.textContent = on ? 'Close' : 'Contents';
  if (on) reveal();
}
button.addEventListener('click', () => open(!nav.classList.contains('open')));
nav.addEventListener('click', (e) => { if ((e.target as Element).closest('a')) open(false); });
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  if (nav.classList.contains('open') && !nav.contains(e.target as Node) && !button.contains(e.target as Node)) open(false);
});
document.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {
  if (e.key === 'Escape' && nav.classList.contains('open')) { open(false); button.focus(); }
});
</script>

